WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.179
A use-after-free condition happens when
code continues to use memory or an object

2
00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:05.858
after its valid lifetime has ended.

3
00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:09.830
In manual-memory environments, this class
of mistake has caused serious reliability

4
00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.589
and security failures because stale
references can point to data that is no longer

5
00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:15.108
valid for the original purpose.

6
00:00:15.188 --> 00:00:17.321
The defensive concept is lifecycle
integrity.

7
00:00:17.401 --> 00:00:21.079
A value is created, used, possibly shared
or borrowed, and eventually cleaned up.

8
00:00:21.159 --> 00:00:24.678
If some part of the program keeps acting
as though the value is still valid after

9
00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:28.330
cleanup, the program is no longer
reasoning about the object that it thinks it is

10
00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:28.730
using.

11
00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.689
Rust changes the default model by making
ownership, borrowing,

12
00:00:31.769 --> 00:00:33.368
and lifetimes part of the language.

13
00:00:33.448 --> 00:00:37.020
Safe Rust prevents ordinary references
from outliving the values they borrow.

14
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.672
It also prevents moved values from being
used as though ownership still exists.

15
00:00:40.752 --> 00:00:43.738
Those rules turn many stale-access risks
into compiler feedback.

16
00:00:43.818 --> 00:00:46.590
This does not mean every Rust boundary is
automatically safe.

17
00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.069
Risk can re-enter through unsafe code, raw
pointers,

18
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:52.614
foreign function interfaces, external
libraries, callback registration,

19
00:00:52.694 --> 00:00:56.426
allocators, and low-level abstractions
where the compiler cannot fully check the

20
00:00:56.506 --> 00:00:57.413
lifetime contract.

21
00:00:57.493 --> 00:00:59.145
Those areas need deliberate review.

22
00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.784
Use-after-free prevention in Rust is
lifecycle design.

23
00:01:01.864 --> 00:01:02.984
Know who owns each value.

24
00:01:03.064 --> 00:01:04.077
Know who may borrow it.

25
00:01:04.157 --> 00:01:05.383
Know when cleanup happens.

26
00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:07.383
Know where references, callbacks, tasks,

27
00:01:07.463 --> 00:01:10.075
and external code can outlive the scope
that created them.

28
00:01:10.155 --> 00:01:13.780
The safe path should make invalid lifetime
relationships impossible or at least

29
00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:14.340
explicit.
